  <a href="#section-why" id="section-why" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Why MIME structure and interoperability matter</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        A plain-text message is simple. Add an HTML alternative, attachments, embedded images, and calendar content, and the exact MIME structure starts to matter.
      </p>
      <p>
        Test the same message in desktop, web, and mobile clients and malformed MIME often shows up in different ways. Symptoms include:
      </p>
      <ul class="indent paragraph">
        <li>not seeing embedded images, or</li>
        <li>icon for attachment is shown while there are no attachments, or</li>
        <li>embedded images are displayed as attachments,</li>
        <li>not seeing the HTML version if a text version is present,</li>
        <li>not seeing the text version if a HTML version is present,</li>
        <li>not seeing attachments,</li>
        <li>attachments aren't handled correctly according to mime type, or</li>
        <li>header information is missing or incorrect so you can't see how long a download will take,</li>
        <li>from/to addresses being malformed (like missing the <code class="inline language-text">&lt;name&gt;</code> part in <code
          class="inline language-text">&lt;name&gt; address@domain.com</code>)
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>Simple Java Mail generates standards-oriented MIME structures for the content you declare and covers the common multipart combinations in its tests.
        Rendering and policy still vary by receiving client and server, so no library can promise identical presentation everywhere.</p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-how" id="section-how" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>How Simple Java Mail builds interoperable messages</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>Simple Java Mail improves <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MIME">MIME interoperability</a> in two ways:</p>
      <ol class="indent paragraph">
        <li>It is built on <a href="https://jakartaee.github.io/mail-api/">Jakarta Mail</a>, Java's standard mail API. Jakarta Mail implements the
          protocols and message formats defined by the relevant RFCs, including SMTP, POP3, and IMAP. Simple Java Mail focuses on outbound SMTP and
          message composition.
        </li>
        <li>Simple Java Mail maps the content you declare to an appropriate multipart structure. It builds the usual
          <code>MimeMessage</code>, <code>BodyPart</code> and <code>MimeMultipart</code> objects so your code can focus on the message itself.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>


